{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A small team should adopt an existing experiment tracker only if their workflow is strictly observational; however, if the research involves active learning loops where the tracker must function as a real-time controller modifying experimental parameters, they must build a custom minimal tracker to avoid the 'integration tax' of forcing a causal control mechanism into a passive logging tool.","counterconditions":["The team lacks the engineering bandwidth to maintain even a minimal custom state machine.","The active learning loop operates on a timescale slow enough to tolerate the latency of external API calls to a managed service.","The experimental parameters can be decoupled from the tracking logic via a robust middleware layer that already exists."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism relies on the distinction between 'passive observation' and 'active control.' Existing tools are architected as databases with UIs (passive), lacking the low-latency, bidirectional API hooks required for control theory applications. Attempting to wrap such tools introduces an 'integration tax'—hidden engineering costs to hack plugins or manage state synchronization—which creates a 'migration cliff' where early unstructured scripting prevents clean data schema adoption later. A custom build enforces a 'mechanist' causal model from day one, treating the tracker as the state machine itself rather than a log of states, thereby eliminating the single point of failure associated with mismatched abstractions.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65b653bf158ecb0"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65b653bf158ecb0"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}